expr main [(i hope this email finds you well), (i hope this message finds you well), (hope this email finds you well), (hope this finds you well), (i hope this finds you well)]

let message "A stock opener. A direct greeting reads warmer."
let description "Suggests warmer, clearer wording than `i hope this email finds you well` in emails."
let kind "Style"
let becomes ["I hope you are well"]

# True positives
test "We think i hope this email finds you well the plan is solid." "We think i hope you are well the plan is solid."
test "My manager said i hope this message finds you well the budget." "My manager said i hope you are well the budget."
test "She wrote hope this email finds you well the summary." "She wrote i hope you are well the summary."
test "After the call, hope this finds you well the team agreed." "After the call, i hope you are well the team agreed."
test "Yesterday i hope this finds you well the draft was sent." "Yesterday i hope you are well the draft was sent."
test "We think i hope this email finds you well the plan is solid." "We think i hope you are well the plan is solid."
test "My manager said i hope this message finds you well the budget." "My manager said i hope you are well the budget."
test "She wrote hope this email finds you well the summary." "She wrote i hope you are well the summary."
test "After the call, hope this finds you well the team agreed." "After the call, i hope you are well the team agreed."
test "I hope this email finds you well the plan." "I hope you are well the plan."

# True negatives
allows "I hope you are well."
allows "I hope this works."
allows "I hope the email arrives."
allows "She hopes you are well."
allows "This email is about the budget."
allows "Hope you find it useful."
allows "The report is ready."
